# .dockerignore: what `docker build .` may send to the builder (the "build context").
#
# Why this file is a SECURITY boundary, not just a speed-up:
# `docker build .` packs up the directory and sends it to the builder BEFORE
# any Dockerfile line runs. Whatever is sent can end up in an image layer
# through a careless `COPY . .`, even if Git ignores it. `.gitignore` only
# decides what Git TRACKS. It has no effect on Docker. This repo's real
# `.env` (API keys) is gitignored, and without this file it would still be
# sent to the builder on every build.
#
# Style: an ALLOW-LIST. Step 1 excludes everything, step 2 lets back in only
# what the running service needs, and step 3 re-excludes dangerous files
# INSIDE the allowed folders. A new file someone drops in the repo root
# tomorrow (a `secrets.json`, a 2 GB model dump) stays out by default. That
# is "fail closed", the same rule as `ROLE_CLEARANCE` in `src/api.py`: what
# nobody listed stays hidden.
#
# Rule order matters: when several lines match a path, the LAST one wins.
#
# The Dockerfile and this file are still sent to the builder (it needs them
# to run the build), but `COPY` never copies them into the image.

# --- Step 1: exclude everything -------------------------------------------
# This alone already keeps out every category the image must not contain:
#   .env, .env.local          secrets (OpenRouter, LangSmith, Tavily keys)
#   .venv/                    the host's environment: macOS/arm64 wheels and host paths
#   .git/                     the full history, including anything ever committed by mistake
#   tests/, docs/             not needed to SERVE requests (docs/traces/ holds trace rows)
#   .pytest_cache/, .ruff_cache/, .deepeval/, .claude/    local tool state
#   data/corpus_v0/           the old corpus; the service reads only corpus_v1
*

# --- Step 2: allow back only what the image needs -------------------------
# The dependency contract: uv installs exactly what uv.lock pins.
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
# The application code: `uvicorn api:app --app-dir src` imports from here.
!src/
# The data the service loads at startup: the 34 documents
# (`procurement_kb.jsonl`) and the 93 labeled queries (`example_queries.jsonl`).
!data/corpus_v1/

# --- Step 3: re-exclude what must never enter, even inside allowed folders --
# Step 2 re-allowed whole folders, so a `src/.env` or a stale cache inside
# them would slip back in without these lines.

# Secrets, anywhere in the tree. (No `.env.example` exists today. If one is
# added, it must hold variable names and safe defaults only, and would be
# re-allowed here with its own `!` line.)
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Python bytecode built on the host (macOS, host paths). The image compiles
# its own (`UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE` in the Dockerfile).
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]

# Packaging metadata left by an old editable install on the host
# (`src/procurerag.egg-info/`). The service does not use it.
**/*.egg-info

# A virtual environment, in case one is ever created inside an allowed folder.
**/.venv

# macOS Finder metadata.
**/.DS_Store
